    followUp: "Review in 2 weeks: assess ear dryness and response to ciprofloxacin drops. If dry: continue monitoring for 3 months for recurrence. If recurrence: repeat culture and sensitivity, consider alternative topical agent (ofloxacin 0.3% drops). Audiometry at 3 months to document hearing status. Referral to ENT surgeon for tympanoplasty evaluation if perforation persists or if conductive hearing loss is significant. Annual ENT review for patients with recurrent CSOM.",
    pearls: "CSOM is classified as tubotympanic (safe) type with central perforation or atticoantral (unsafe) type with cholesteatoma. Tubotympanic CSOM carries lower risk of complications; atticoantral type is more dangerous (bone erosion, intracranial complications). Topical fluoroquinolone ear drops (ciprofloxacin, ofloxacin) are the mainstay of treatment — they achieve high concentrations in the middle ear with minimal systemic absorption. Aural toilet (dry mopping) before drop instillation is essential for treatment success — drops placed on a discharging ear without cleaning first will not penetrate. Oral antibiotics have limited evidence in CSOM unless there is systemic involvement. The most common organism in CSOM is Pseudomonas aeruginosa (40-70% of cases). Tympanoplasty should only be performed when the ear has been completely dry for at least 3 months.",
