    followUp: "Review in 5-7 days to assess clinical response. If improving: complete 5-7 day antibiotic course, continue saline irrigation for 2-4 weeks. If not improving: CT paranasal sinuses and ENT referral for possible endoscopic sinus surgery or culture-guided therapy. If recurrent sinusitis (3+ episodes per year): investigate for predisposing factors (allergic rhinitis, nasal polyps, immunodeficiency, dental infection) and consider ENT referral. Allergy testing if allergic rhinitis suspected.",
    pearls: "The 'double-sickening' pattern is classic for acute bacterial sinusitis — initial viral URI improves, then suddenly worsens after 5-7 days as bacteria colonise the inflamed sinuses. Most acute sinusitis is viral (90-98%); antibiotics are only indicated when bacterial infection is suspected (symptoms >10 days without improvement, severe symptoms from onset, or worsening after initial improvement). Amoxicillin-clavulanate is preferred over plain amoxicillin because 20-40% of H. influenzae and 90% of M. catarrhalis produce beta-lactamases. Topical decongestants (oxymetazoline, xylometazoline) should NEVER be used for more than 3-5 days — prolonged use causes rhinitis medicamentosa. Saline nasal irrigation is one of the most effective adjunctive treatments — it improves mucociliary clearance, thins secretions, and reduces bacterial load.",
